<title>JAPAN: Japan GSP copper, zinc imports rise in 1997/98.</title>
<headline>Japan GSP copper, zinc imports rise in 1997/98.</headline>
<dateline>TOKYO 1997-04-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>Japan's imports of copper and zinc under the Generalised System of Preferences (GSP) scheme rose in fiscal 1997/98, while lead imports dropped, the Finance Ministry said on Wednesday.</p>
<p>The GSP scheme grants tariff exemptions to developing country imports submitted for customs clearance at the beginning of the fiscal year on April 1.</p>
<p>Metal import figures under the GSP scheme in 1997/98 were as follows (in tonnes):</p>
<p>COPPER</p>
<p>				1997/98	  1996/97</p>
<p>    China			846		 nil</p>
<p>    Philippines	    5,550	    12,483</p>
<p>    Iran		     1,508		 nil</p>
<p>    Oman		     1,361	     1,516</p>
<p>    Mexico		     299		 nil</p>
<p>    Peru		    11,035	    12,101</p>
<p>    Chile		   47,394	    18,081</p>
<p>    South Africa	     379	     4,889</p>
<p>    Zambia		   7,259	    14,576</p>
<p>    TOTAL		   75,631	    63,886</p>
<p>ZINC</p>
<p>				1997/98	  1996/97</p>
<p>    South Korea	    2,349		 nil</p>
<p>    China		   89,669	    48,868</p>
<p>    Taiwan		     100		 500</p>
<p>    Kazakhstan		 207	     4,982</p>
<p>    Poland		   1,354		 nil</p>
<p>    Serbia/Montenegro	475		 nil</p>
<p>    Bulgaria		 3,360	     5,465</p>
<p>    Turkey		     898		 nil</p>
<p>    Macedonia		3,638	     2,608</p>
<p>    Mexico		     248		 928</p>
<p>    Peru		    10,340	     9,115</p>
<p>    Brazil		     nil	    10,770</p>
<p>    Argentina		  nil		 299</p>
<p>    TOTAL		  112,639	    83,535</p>
<p>LEAD</p>
<p>				1997/98	  1996/97</p>
<p>    South Korea	    1,000		 nil</p>
<p>    China		    7,048	     8,931</p>
<p>    Mexico		   1,595		 400</p>
<p>    Peru		     4,474	     6,121</p>
<p>    TOTAL		   14,117	    15,452</p>
<p>-- Tokyo Commodity Desk +81-3-3432-6179</p>
